    {
      "title": "ANOVA inputs are supplied",
      "quote": "We'll just give you the sample mean and sample standard deviations.",
      "detail": "Practise converting supplied SDs to variances and building within/between estimates, rather than spending the limited exam route repeatedly averaging raw ANOVA observations."
    },
    {
      "title": "Balanced ANOVA",
      "quote": "it will be a so-called balanced study, the same number of subjects in each group",
      "detail": "Use the balanced variance recipe. Do not spend this midterm route on hand sum-of-squares ANOVA."
    },
    {
      "title": "R: use output, do not write programs",
      "quote": "You're not going to have to write any code.",
      "detail": "Read supplied ANOVA, t-test, or chi-square output; locate within variance/df/statistics/p and use them for the requested continuation or pairwise decisions."
    },
    {
      "title": "Always apply the 2\u00d72 correction",
      "quote": "So you should always use this correction.",
      "detail": "Use Yates for the course\u2019s 2\u00d72 z/chi-square comparisons. Larger contingency tables use ordinary gaps."
    },
    {
      "title": "Population conclusions and appropriate methods",
      "quote": "our focus in this class is to try to say something about a population",
      "detail": "Choose the test using data type and design; state the population null; calculate; use the matching table/df; conclude in the question\u2019s context."
    },
    {
      "title": "Follow-up comparisons",
      "quote": "you have to do each one at a smaller level",
      "detail": "After an overall difference, identify the requested pair family; use Bonferroni or Holm, preserving the ANOVA noise/df for mean comparisons."
    },
    {
      "title": "Association size and design",
      "quote": "how big is that thing?",
      "detail": "Distinguish significance from magnitude. Use risk ratios for prospective risks and odds ratios for case-control odds."
    },
    {
